# Should natural monopolies be regulated or owned by the state?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Price caps, rate of return, public ownership What goes wrong with each?"
next: {"title":"How much should a firm produce?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/113-how-much-should-a-firm-produce.cast"}

## Sold with no debt
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Debt 1989
      - Dividends 1990–2023
      - Debt 2023
    values:
      - 0
      - 53
      - 60.3
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      prefix: £
      suffix: bn
      decimals: 0
    colors:
      - red
      - orange
      - red
    y_label: £ billion, England's water companies
    label_size: 26
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 70
    box:
      x: 90
      'y': 150
      w: 540
      h: 460
sources:
    - id: guardian2023
      title: How much of your water bill is swallowed up by company debt?
      authors: The Guardian (analysis of company annual reports)
      year: 2023
      finding: England's privatised water companies went from no debt in 1989 to a combined £60.3bn of debt in 2023, and paid about £53bn in dividends between 1990 and 2023.
    - id: ea2024
      title: 'Event Duration Monitoring: storm overflow annual returns for 2024'
      authors: Environment Agency
      year: 2025
      finding: Storm overflows in England spilled for about 3.61 million hours in 2024 (a record, 0.2% more than 2023), with spill counts down 2.9%.
      url: https://www.gov.uk/government/news/environment-agency-storm-overflow-spill-data-for-2024
    - id: euronews2025
      title: UK to create new water regulator after crisis at Thames Water
      authors: Euronews
      year: 2025
      finding: In July 2025 the UK government said it would abolish Ofwat after the Cunliffe review; Thames Water, with about £17bn of debt, had teetered on the brink of nationalisation.
      url: https://www.euronews.com/2025/07/21/uk-to-create-new-water-regulator-after-crisis-at-thames-water
    - id: statnett
      title: Statnett
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2025
      finding: Statnett, Norway's transmission system operator (about 11,000 km of high-voltage lines), was split from Statskraftverkene in 1992 and is wholly owned by the Norwegian state.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Statnett

    text sew "3.6m hours of sewage spills, 2024" #b5482e cites ["ea2024"] x 820 y 470 width 300 font_size 32 hidden true
    text thames "Thames Water: £17bn debt" #b5482e cites ["euronews2025"] x 820 y 500 width 300 font_size 32 hidden true
    text ofwat "Ofwat to go" #b5482e cites ["euronews2025"] x 820 y 420 width 300 font_size 32 hidden true
    text nor "Norway: Statnett, state-owned grid" #2f6b8f cites ["statnett"] x 820 y 470 width 300 font_size 32 hidden true
    row houses gap 70 fit.h 120 fit.w 740 fit.x 130 fit.y 530 hidden true
    icon h1 size 90 set twemoji icon_look picture of house in houses hidden true
    icon h2 size 90 set twemoji icon_look picture of house in houses hidden true
    icon h3 size 90 set twemoji icon_look picture of house in houses hidden true
    icon h4 size 90 set twemoji icon_look picture of house in houses hidden true
    shape street #8f887c shape rect x 500 y 495 width 860 height 44 style.fill #d9d3c7 hidden true
    text street_l "street" #8f887c x 500 y 495 font_size 24 hidden true
    shape pipeA #2f6b8f shape rect x 500 y 410 width 860 height 40 radius 14 style.fill #9cc3dc hidden true
    text pipeA_l "pipe A" #3d3833 x 500 y 410 font_size 30 hidden true
    shape pipeB #2f6b8f shape rect x 500 y 335 width 860 height 40 radius 14 style.fill #9cc3dc hidden true
    text pipeB_l "pipe B" #3d3833 x 500 y 335 font_size 30 hidden true
    mark strikeB around pipeB #b5482e kind strike hidden true
    text nm "natural monopoly" #b5482e x 500 y 250 font_size 52 hidden true
    text q1 "Who owns it?" x 280 y 170 font_size 40 hidden true
    text q2 "Who holds its price down?" x 680 y 170 font_size 40 hidden true

    card title "Sold with no debt"

Should t (@draw axes@)he pipes that bring your water belong to the state, or to a private firm the state keeps an eye on? England chose the firm, and sold its water companies  (@draw bar_1 ends@)in 1989.

B: With no debt at all. By 2023, a Guardian count foun (@draw bar_3@)d the English water companies owed about sixty billion pounds.

Over the same years the (@draw bar_2@)y paid their owners about fifty-three billion in dividends. Alm (@highlight bar_2 bar_3 effect box@)ost as much as they now owe.

B: And in 2024, their storm overflow (@draw sew@)s spilled sewage into rivers and seas for about three point six million hours.

    pause 0.3

    erase sew

In 2025 the biggest, Thames Water, wi (@draw thames@)th about seventeen billion pounds of debt, teetered on the edge of state control. The government said it woul (@draw ofwat@)d abolish the water regulator, Ofwat.

    erase thames ofwat

B: Norway runs its main power grid  (@draw nor@)the other way: it belongs to the state, through a company called Statnett.

B: So the answer's easy. State ownership. No shareholders to pay.
    highlight nor effect underline

    quiz "Who should own a town's water pipes?" judge false
        intro: Maybe. But first, your own guess, before any theory.
        right: Hold that guess. First: why only one firm at all?
        * The state
        * A private firm, with a regulator
        * It matters less than you think

    erase axes bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 nor

Why not jus (@draw houses street street_l pipeA pipeA_l@)t let water firms compete, like bakeries? Because nobody wants two se (@draw pipeB pipeB_l@)ts of pipes under the same street.

B: One network serve (@draw strikeB@)s the whole town more  (@highlight pipeA effect box@)cheaply than two: a natural monopo (@draw nm@)ly, the giants' story taken to its end.

So a monopoly is coming either way. The only  (@draw q1@)questions are who owns it, and who h (@draw q2@)olds its price down.

    pause 0.4

## Every drop at cost
domain: {"x":[0,1000],"y":[0,11]}
vars:
    p:
      value: 2
      min: 2
      max: 5
      step: 0.1
      label: Price ($ per m³)
    prof:
      expr: (p-2)*100*(10-p) - 1200
    loss:
      expr: max(0, 1200 - (p-2)*100*(10-p))
sources:
    - id: hotelling1938
      title: The General Welfare in Relation to Problems of Taxation and of Railway and Utility Rates
      authors: Harold Hotelling
      year: 1938
      finding: 'Econometrica 6(3): argued that utilities should price at marginal cost and cover their fixed costs from general taxation.'
    - id: coase1946
      title: The Marginal Cost Controversy
      authors: Ronald H. Coase
      year: 1946
      finding: 'Economica 13(51): against Hotelling, proposed multi-part pricing — a fixed charge to cover the fixed costs plus a price per unit equal to marginal cost — which also tests whether consumers value the network enough to pay for it.'

    axes axes x_label "Q, m³ a day" y_label "$ per m³ (P)" cross corner ticks.x [200,400,600,800] ticks.y [2,4,6,8,10] hidden true
    curve D #b5482e expr "10 - 0.01*x" x_from 0 x_to 1000 hidden true
    label label_D "D" right #b5482e attach_to D hidden true
    curve MC #2f6b8f expr "2 + 0*x" x_from 0 x_to 1000 hidden true
    label label_MC "MC" right #2f6b8f attach_to MC hidden true
    curve ATC #8a5fa8 expr "2 + 1200/x" x_from 135 x_to 1000 hidden true
    label label_ATC "ATC" right #8a5fa8 attach_to ATC hidden true
    dot m #b5482e at.on D at.x 400 hidden true
    curve pm #8f887c expr "6 + 0*x" x_from 0 x_to 400 dash true hidden true
    text label_pm "$6" #b5482e at.data [440,6.6] font_size 34 hidden true
    dot e #3d6b3a at.on D at.x 800 hidden true
    text label_e "800 m³" #3d6b3a at.data [860,3] font_size 32 hidden true
    curve atc800 #8a5fa8 expr "3.5 + 0*x" x_from 0 x_to 800 dash true hidden true
    region loss x_from 0 x_to 800 between ["MC","atc800"] style.fill #c96567 hidden true
    text label_loss "−$1,200 a day" #3d3833 at.data [400,2.75] font_size 32 hidden true
    curve pl expr "p + 0*x" x_from 0 x_to 1000 style.opacity 0 hidden true
    curve atcp expr "2 + 12/(10-p) + 0*x" x_from 0 x_to 1000 style.opacity 0 hidden true
    dot pt #3d3833 at.on D at.x 800 radius 10 bind {"at.x":"100*(10-p)"} hidden true
    region gapL x_from 0 x_to.ref pt between ["pl","atcp"] style.fill #c96567 hidden true
    text readout "Price ${p:2}  ·  loss ${loss:0,} a day" at.data [640,9.6] font_size 32 hidden true
    region dwl x_from 600 x_to 800 between ["MC","D"] style.fill #87a878 hidden true
    text w1 "1  Taxes pay the pipes" at.data [790,9.6] cites ["hotelling1938"] font_size 34 hidden true
    text w2 "2  Price = average cost" at.data [790,8.6] font_size 34 hidden true
    text w3 "3  Fee + $2 a m³" at.data [790,7.6] cites ["coase1946"] font_size 34 hidden true
    mark ring3 around w3 #3d6b3a kind box hidden true
    text coasecue "Coase, 1946" #3d6b3a at.data [790,6.6] cites ["coase1946"] font_size 30 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch mth top-right work [{"tex":"P = 10 - 0.01Q = 2 \\Rightarrow Q = 800"},{"tex":"ATC = 2 + \\frac{1200}{800} = 3.50"},{"tex":"\\text{loss} = (3.50 - 2)\\times 800 = 1200 = F"},{"tex":"10 - 0.01Q = 2 + \\frac{1200}{Q} \\Rightarrow Q = 600,\\ P = 4"}] font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Every drop at cost"

B: Every fix start (@draw axes D label_D@)s from the same picture. Take Harbourtown's water, with illustrative numbers: here is what the town would pay for each cubic metre.

B: Pumping one more cubic  (@draw MC label_MC@)metre costs two dollars. That's the marginal cost, MC.

B: But the pipes cost twelve hundred dollars a day, however much w (@draw ATC label_ATC@)ater flows. Spread over more water, the average cost, ATC, just keeps falling.

Left alone, the owner behaves like any monopolist: f (@draw m pm label_pm@)our hundred cubic metres a day, at six dollars each.

So the regulator says: (@fade m pm label_pm to 0.3@) charge what the water costs to suppl (@highlight MC effect underline@)y. Two dollars.

B: Then the town takes eight hundr (@draw e label_e@)ed cubic metres, and every one is worth at least what it costs. That's the efficient amount.

    pause 0.3

So where's the catch?
    highlight ATC

B: At eight hundred cubic m (@draw atc800@)etres the average cost is three dollars fifty. Sold at two, the water l (@draw loss label_loss@)oses twelve hundred dollars a day: the whole cost of the pipes.

B: When average cost falls, marginal cost lies below it. Price at marginal cost and you can never pay for the pipes. That's the dilemma.
    highlight ATC MC

    erase loss label_loss atc800

    draw pt gapL readout

Try it: drag the price up from two dollars, and find where the loss disappears.
    explore params ["p"]

B: At four dollars it breaks even, on six hundred cubic metres. But the two hundred cubic metres no l (@draw dwl@)onger bought were each worth more than the two dollars they cost.
    animate p 4 duration 2.5 ghost true

    erase readout gapL dwl

    erase m pm label_pm

So three ways out. Taxes pay (@draw w1@) for the pipes, as Harold Hotelling argued in 1938. Or t (@draw w2@)he price covers average cost. Or th (@draw w3@)e bill splits in two.

B: The split (@draw coasecue@) was Ronald Coase's answer, in 1946: a fixed fee pays for the (@draw ring3@) pipes, and each cubic metre costs its two dollars. Say four hundred households: three dollars a day each.

    erase coasecue w1 w2 w3 ring3 pt

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own
        intro: Want the numbers worked out?

@math

    erase mathq

    fade axes D label_D MC label_MC ATC label_ATC e label_e to 0.15

Demand is ten minus a hundredth of Q. At a price of two, Q is eight hundred.
    draw mth_box mth_line_1

Average cost there: two plus twelve hundred over eight hundred, three fifty.
    draw mth_line_2

B: So the loss is a dollar fifty times eight hundred: twelve hundred, exactly the fixed cost F.
    draw mth_line_3

B: Set price equal to average cost and the only market-sized answer is six hundred cubic metres at four dollars.
    draw mth_line_4

    erase mth

@test

    erase mathq

    fade axes D label_D MC label_MC ATC label_ATC e label_e to 1

    pause 0.3

## Who sets the price?
sources:
    - id: aj1962
      title: Behavior of the Firm Under Regulatory Constraint
      authors: Harvey Averch and Leland L. Johnson
      year: 1962
      finding: 'American Economic Review 52(5): 1052–1069. A firm allowed a rate of return on capital above its cost of capital uses more capital than the cost-minimising amount.'
    - id: littlechild1983
      title: Regulation of British Telecommunications' Profitability
      authors: Stephen Littlechild
      year: 1983
      finding: Report to the UK government (published February 1983) recommending an RPI − X price cap instead of rate-of-return control; BT's first cap at privatisation in 1984 was RPI − 3.
      url: https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1983/feb/14/british-telecom-report
    - id: demsetz1968
      title: Why Regulate Utilities?
      authors: Harold Demsetz
      year: 1968
      finding: 'Journal of Law and Economics 11(1): competition for the market — bidding for the right to serve — can discipline a natural monopoly without rate regulation.'
    - id: williamson1976
      title: Franchise Bidding for Natural Monopolies — in General and with Respect to CATV
      authors: Oliver E. Williamson
      year: 1976
      finding: 'Bell Journal of Economics 7(1): 73–104. Long franchise contracts under uncertainty need renegotiation and monitoring; the incumbent''s advantage at renewal means franchise bidding can end up with regulation''s own problems (the Oakland cable TV case).'
    - id: bel2010
      title: Is private production of public services cheaper than public production? A meta-regression analysis of solid waste and water services
      authors: Germà Bel, Xavier Fageda and Mildred E. Warner
      year: 2010
      finding: 'Journal of Policy Analysis and Management 29(3): 553–577. Across all econometric studies of water distribution and solid waste collection, no systematic support for lower costs with private production.'
      url: https://econpapers.repec.org/RePEc:wly:jpamgt:v:29:y:2010:i:3:p:553-577
    - id: rme
      title: Economic regulation of network companies
      authors: NVE / RME (Norwegian Energy Regulatory Authority)
      year: 2025
      finding: Each grid company, including the state-owned TSO Statnett, gets an incentive-based revenue cap each year (30% own costs, 70% a benchmarked cost norm); Statnett is benchmarked against other European TSOs.
      url: https://www.nve.no/norwegian-energy-regulatory-authority/economic-regulation/

    text ghost_note "P = MC + fee" #8f887c x 330 y 625 font_size 40 hidden true
    box n_ror "Rate of return" width 270 height 90 radius 12 font_size 32 in grid hidden true
    box n_cap "Price cap RPI−X" width 270 height 90 radius 12 font_size 32 in grid hidden true
    box n_auc "Franchise auction" width 270 height 90 radius 12 font_size 32 in grid hidden true
    box n_pub "Public ownership" width 270 height 90 radius 12 font_size 32 in grid hidden true
    grid grid gap 90 columns 2 walk true fit.h 370 fit.w 620 fit.x 30 fit.y 200 hidden true
    text c_ror "overbuilds" below n_ror gap 12 #b5482e cites ["aj1962"] font_size 30 hidden true
    text c_cap "skimps" below n_cap gap 12 #b5482e font_size 30 hidden true
    text c_auc "renegotiates" below n_auc gap 12 #b5482e cites ["williamson1976"] font_size 30 hidden true
    text c_pub "same two questions" below n_pub gap 12 #b5482e font_size 30 hidden true
    text r_ror "allowed: 8% on capital" x 830 y 470 width 290 font_size 32 hidden true
    text r_cap "BT, 1984: RPI − 3" cites ["littlechild1983"] x 830 y 470 width 290 font_size 34 hidden true
    portrait demsetz cites ["demsetz1968"] x 830 y 480 width 190 of "Harold Demsetz" cameo true hidden true
    text want "wants low costs?" #2f6b8f x 830 y 500 font_size 36 hidden true
    text know "knows the costs?" #2f6b8f x 830 y 420 font_size 36 hidden true
    text bel "Private: no systematic saving (Bel et al., 2010)" cites ["bel2010"] x 830 y 300 width 300 font_size 28 hidden true
    text nor "Statnett: state-owned AND capped" #2f6b8f cites ["rme"] x 830 y 300 width 300 font_size 28 hidden true
    text st1 "mostly agreed: control it" #2f6b8f x 780 y 630 font_size 34 hidden true
    text st2 "contested: whose hand" #b5482e x 780 y 585 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "Who sets the price?"

Coase's spli (@draw ghost_note@)t is clean on paper. But someone has to set the fee and the  (@fade ghost_note to 0.35@)price, and that someone needs to know the costs.

B: America's answer was rat (@draw n_ror@)e-of-return regulation: the firm may earn, say, eight p (@draw r_ror@)ercent a year on its capital, and no more.

In 1962 Harvey Averch and Leland Johnson saw the flaw. If the allowed return beats the cost of borrowing, every extra pip (@draw c_ror@)e is profit, so the firm overbuilds. And whatever it spends, cus (@highlight n_ror effect box@)tomers pay back.

    erase r_ror

B: Britain tried the opposite. In 1983 Stephe (@draw n_cap@)n Littlechild proposed RPI minus X: prices may rise by inflation minus X percent, whatever costs do (@draw r_cap@). BT got RPI minus three.

So cut a cost and you keep the saving until the next review. A strong incentive. But it also pays to skim (@draw c_cap@)p on what the regulator can't easily see, and critics say (@highlight c_cap effect underline@) English water did just that.

    erase r_cap

B: Harold Demse (@draw n_auc demsetz@)tz asked in 1968: why regulate at all? Auction the right to be the monopoly to whoever offers the lowest price.

    erase demsetz

Oliver Williamson's reply: over twenty years things change, and once the rivals have gone home the w (@draw c_auc@)inner renegotiates. The auction turns into reg (@highlight n_auc effect box@)ulation by contract.

B: And public o (@draw n_pub@)wnership drops the shareholders (@draw c_pub@), not the problems.

Every option asks the same two things: does whoever sets  (@draw want@)the price want low costs, and does (@draw know@) it know them? Wanting is the incentive problem, knowing the information problem (@highlight n_ror n_cap n_auc n_pub@), and a regulator can be captured by the firm it watches.

B: The evidence: in 2010, Germà Bel and two colleagu (@draw bel@)es pooled the studies of private and public water and rubbish services. Private firms showed no systematic saving.

    erase bel

And Norway mixes both: state-own (@draw nor@)ed Statnett still has its revenue capped by a regulator, which compares it with other European grids. That a natural monopoly nee (@draw st1@)ds a firm hand: mostly agreed. W (@draw st2@)hose hand: contested.

B: So, regulated or owned? Ownership changes who answers the two questions, not the questions.
    highlight want know

    quiz "A ski lift costs a lot to build, but almost nothing per extra ride. Which pricing covers its cost without turning skiers away?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: A season pass plus a low price per ride: the pass pays for the lift, and no skier is turned away from a ride that costs almost nothing.
        wrong: A per-ride price far above its near-zero cost turns skiers away: here, a season pass plus a low price per ride.
        + A season pass, plus a low price per ride
        * A price per ride that covers the average cost
        * A high price per ride, and close on quiet days

    quiz "A city auctions a 15-year bus franchise to the lowest fare bid. In year 3 the winner says fuel costs rose and asks for higher fares; no rival can step in quickly. Whose prediction is this?" voice b
        right: Williamson's: with the rivals gone home, the winner can renegotiate, and the contract becomes regulation by another name.
        wrong: Ask who holds the cards once the other bidders have gone home: here, Williamson's renegotiation.
        + Williamson's: the auction turns into renegotiation
        * Demsetz's: bidding keeps fares low for good
        * Averch and Johnson's: the firm overbuilds

    hide ghost_note grid c_ror c_cap c_auc c_pub want know nor st1 st2

    cards sortq check each
        Incentive problem: Minister freezes prices before an election, Grid builds a substation it doesn't need
        Information problem: Regulator can't spot padded costs, Bidders can't foresee 20 years

    ask "Wanting or knowing? Sort each failure." on sortq store srt stage own
        right: All four: the freeze and the substation are wanting; padded costs and 20 years ahead are knowing.
        wrong: Would wanting harder fix it, or only knowing more? Here, the freeze and the substation are wanting; padded costs and 20 years, knowing.

    show ghost_note grid c_ror c_cap c_auc c_pub want know st1 st2

B: Whoever holds the pipes, someone must want low costs, and know them.
    highlight want know effect underline

    pause 0.4

