      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1rbo8zl'
    '|a||Left alone, the owner behaves like any monopolist: four hundred cubic metres a day, at six dollars each.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7000
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:ax0qhv'
    '|a||So the regulator says: charge what the water costs to supply. Two dollars.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5880
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1c7l40p'
    '|b||Then the town takes eight hundred cubic metres, and every one is worth at least what it costs. That''s the efficient amount.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7000
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:bttm8h'
    '|a||So where''s the catch?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2560
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:5jt5uf'
    '|b||At eight hundred cubic metres the average cost is three dollars fifty. Sold at two, the water loses twelve hundred dollars a day: the whole cost of the pipes.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9640
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1i0oyfu'
    '|b||When average cost falls, marginal cost lies below it. Price at marginal cost and you can never pay for the pipes. That''s the dilemma.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8120
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:8oa18i'
    '|b||At four dollars it breaks even, on six hundred cubic metres. But the two hundred cubic metres no longer bought were each worth more than the two dollars they cost.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10840
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|a||So three ways out. Taxes pay for the pipes, as Harold Hotelling argued in 1938. Or the price covers average cost. Or the bill splits in two.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11800
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:brg8tc'
    '|b||The split was Ronald Coase''s answer, in 1946: a fixed fee pays for the pipes, and each cubic metre costs its two dollars. Say four hundred households: three dollars a day each.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11280
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:xkt6n2'
    '|a||Want the numbers worked out? Show the math, or skip it?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3360
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1s84bs3'
    '|a||Demand is ten minus a hundredth of Q. At a price of two, Q is eight hundred.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6840
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|a||Average cost there: two plus twelve hundred over eight hundred, three fifty.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5920
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1owj623'
    '|b||So the loss is a dollar fifty times eight hundred: twelve hundred, exactly the fixed cost F.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7560
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:a0cu76'
    '|b||Set price equal to average cost and the only market-sized answer is six hundred cubic metres at four dollars.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7680
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:pqlqk5'
    '|a||Coase''s split is clean on paper. But someone has to set the fee and the price, and that someone needs to know the costs.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8440
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||America''s answer was rate-of-return regulation: the firm may earn, say, eight percent a year on its capital, and no more.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9120
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:13669hp'
    '|a||In 1962 Harvey Averch and Leland Johnson saw the flaw. If the allowed return beats the cost of borrowing, every extra pipe is profit, so the firm overbuilds. And whatever it spends, customers pay back.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 14120
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1hx8x81'
    '|b||Britain tried the opposite. In 1983 Stephen Littlechild proposed RPI minus X: prices may rise by inflation minus X percent, whatever costs do. BT got RPI minus three.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 13160
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:ra9k9g'
    '|a||So cut a cost and you keep the saving until the next review. A strong incentive. But it also pays to skimp on what the regulator can''t easily see, and critics say English water did just that.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 14200
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:mh0fn5'
    '|b||Harold Demsetz asked in 1968: why regulate at all? Auction the right to be the monopoly to whoever offers the lowest price.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8760
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1lwkwde'
    '|a||Oliver Williamson''s reply: over twenty years things change, and once the rivals have gone home the winner renegotiates. The auction turns into regulation by contract.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11080
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:3039p'
    '|b||And public ownership drops the shareholders, not the problems.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4520
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1j99rm9'
    '|a||Every option asks the same two things: does whoever sets the price want low costs, and does it know them? Wanting is the incentive problem, knowing the information problem, and a regulator can be captured by the firm it watches.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 14280
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:abq5qq'
    '|b||The evidence: in 2010, Germà Bel and two colleagues pooled the studies of private and public water and rubbish services. Private firms showed no systematic saving.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11640
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:8q2nuz'
    '|a||And Norway mixes both: state-owned Statnett still has its revenue capped by a regulator, which compares it with other European grids. That a natural monopoly needs a firm hand: mostly agreed. Whose hand: contested.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 14320
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1vywtii'
    '|b||So, regulated or owned? Ownership changes who answers the two questions, not the questions.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6160
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1lxqxqb'
    '|a||Three new cases. A ski lift costs a lot to build, but almost nothing per extra ride. Which pricing covers its cost without turning skiers away?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9720
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1pmgo8a'
    '|a||A season pass plus a low price per ride: the pass pays for the lift, and no skier is turned away from a ride that costs almost nothing.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9160
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||A city auctions a 15-year bus franchise to the lowest fare bid. In year 3 the winner says fuel costs rose and asks for higher fares; no rival can step in quickly. Whose prediction is this?':
